// Thinking Process:
// 1. High-Yield Concept: Antifungal targets map cleanly onto fungal structures — ergosterol membrane (polyenes bind it; azoles block 14-alpha-demethylase; terbinafine blocks squalene epoxidase); cell wall beta-1,3-glucan (echinocandins); nucleic acid (flucytosine via cytosine permease/deaminase → 5-FU); microtubules (griseofulvin). Selectivity and adverse effects flow from these targets (AMB → cholesterol cross-binding → nephrotoxicity/RTA/K-Mg wasting; azoles → CYP/steroidogenesis/QT; terbinafine → hepatotoxicity; flucytosine → gut bacteria activation → marrow suppression).
// 2. The "Trap": Students conflate which azole works for what — voriconazole = aspergillosis DOC; itraconazole = dimorphic fungi DOC and HF-contraindicated; posaconazole + isavuconazole = ONLY azoles vs. Mucorales; fluconazole = renally cleared (only azole needing renal adjustment) + CSF penetration → cryptococcal consolidation/maintenance. Also: cryptococcal meningitis = AMB + flucytosine induction, NOT azole monotherapy.
// 3. Key Distractor: Mechanism cross-class swaps (calling azole effect echinocandin, calling polyene effect azole), and "right drug wrong patient" — fluconazole monotherapy for aspergillosis, voriconazole for Mucorales, echinocandin for Cryptococcus.
// 4. Vignette Strategy: Q1-10 first-order MOA/AE-mechanism with drug name withheld whenever testing the mechanism (e.g., "the new antifungal class that..."), Q11-20 vignettes (immunocompromised, cryptococcal meningitis, invasive aspergillosis, mucormycosis with DKA, onychomycosis, vulvovaginal candidiasis, oral thrush in infant, histoplasmosis, voriconazole vision AE, itraconazole HF contraindication, fluconazole renal adjustment).
